All that ogygia exposes, by import path, plus each option on the `ogygia()` Vite plugin. Deep pages are linked where a symbol has one.

## `ogygia`

The runtime surface. Import the namespace (`import * as ogygia from 'ogygia'`) or named exports.

| Export          | Kind       | What it is                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |
| --------------- | ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `Boundary`      | component  | Error boundary for island subtrees.                                                                                                                                                                                              |
| `Region`        | component  | Renders a [held region](/docs/regions/held-regions): `<Region of={x} />`.                                                                                                                                                        |
| `region`        | function   | Mints a held region: `region(Component, props)`.                                                                                                                                                                                 |
| `isRegion`      | function   | Type guard for a held region value.                                                                                                                                                                                              |
| `transport`     | hook value | The Kit `transport` entry that lets a held region cross the wire.                                                                                                                                                                |
| `wire`          | macro      | Declare the codec of a transportable class with `static wire = import.meta.og.wire({ encode, decode })`. It is a compile-time macro, not an importable symbol. See [shared state](/docs/data-state/state-context#sharing-state). |
| `script`        | function   | An inline `<script>` string: a self-contained function (`run`), or JSON / JSON-LD / an import map / speculation rules, with typed attributes. See [Pre-hydration scripts](#ogygia-pre-hydration-scripts).                        |
| `preload`       | function   | Warm the HTML of a deferred/live region now, before its binder wakes: `preload(region(…))`. See [held regions](/docs/regions/held-regions).                                                                                      |
| `preference`    | function   | A persisted, site-wide client choice that flips a CSS state with no shipped JS beyond one pre-paint script: the theme / code-language / tabs primitive.                                                                          |
| `createContext` | function   | Typed sugar over a string context key: `createContext('key')`, callable for a `<Provide>` entry, `.get()` to read. See [state & context](/docs/data-state/state-context).                                                        |
| `Provide`       | component  | Provide context values to islands below it across the DOM: `<Provide values={[ctx(v)]}>`.                                                                                                                                        |
| `setContext`    | function   | Drop-in for the `setContext` of Svelte, which also bridges string-keyed values to child islands (the flat page root).                                                                                                            |
| `hydratedBy`    | function   | Which schedule woke this hydration root (for example `'interaction'`). Call it during setup, like `getContext`.                                                                                                                  |

Types: `RegionValue`, `AwaitableRegion`, `InlineRegion`, `DualRegion`, `DeferredRegion`, `TransportCodec`.

### Import attributes

Authored on a component import, not called:

| Attribute         | Makes                                                                        | Values                                                                       |
| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `with { wake }`   | the schedule: when an island's JS runs (or when a `live` region revalidates) | `'load'` · `'idle'` · `'visible'` · `'interaction'` · `'(media)'` · `'none'` |
| `with { fetch }`  | the schedule of a `render: 'deferred'` hole: when its HTML arrives           | `'load'` (default) · `'idle'` · `'visible'` · `'interaction'` · `'(media)'`  |
| `with { render }` | the delivery mode                                                            | `'static'` (default) · `'deferred'` (server island) · `'live'` (revalidates) |
| `with { region }` | a held, server-chosen region marker                                          | `'raw'`                                                                      |
| `with { keep }`   | keep the live island across SPA navigation                                   | a name                                                                       |
| `with { preset }` | a named preset from plugin config                                            | any preset name                                                              |

`render` and `wake` are the two dials: the mode and the schedule. A `render: 'deferred'` hole takes `fetch` for its schedule, not `wake`. The old `render: 'deferred'` + `wake` pairing fails the build. See [Regions](/docs/regions/regions) for the concept.

### Pre-hydration scripts

A csr=false page has no client until islands wake. Thus all that must run **before the first paint** must be a plain inline `<script>` in the HTML. That covers the theme set (no dark-mode flash), the start of a deferred font, or an early flag read. To hand-write that string is awkward. A literal `</script>` inside a Svelte component closes the own script of the component. That is why people reach for `String.fromCharCode(60)` tricks.

`script()` removes that. It takes ONE plain object: exactly one payload key, beside the attributes of the tag. A bare function with trailing args is the shortcut for a plain classic script: `script(fn, a, b)` is `{ run: fn, args: [a, b] }`. You get back a `<script>…</script>` string. It is only a string. Thus `{@html}` it where you want the tag, usually `<svelte:head>`.

```svelte
<script>
  import * as ogygia from 'ogygia';
  import fontUrl from './mono.css?url';
</script>

<svelte:head>
  <!-- No-flash theme, runs before paint (the shortcut) -->
  {@html ogygia.script(() => {
    try {
      const t = localStorage.getItem('theme');
      if (t === 'light' || t === 'dark') document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', t);
    } catch {}
  })}

  <!-- Deferred font: the hashed URL is closed-over data, so pass it in `args` -->
  {@html ogygia.script({
    run: (href) => {
      addEventListener('load', () => {
        const l = document.createElement('link');
        l.rel = 'stylesheet';
        l.href = href;
        document.head.appendChild(l);
      }, { once: true });
    },
    args: [fontUrl],
    type: 'module'
  })}

  <!-- Data: structured data for search engines -->
  {@html ogygia.script({ ld: { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Article', headline } })}
</svelte:head>
```

| Payload key      | Tag                                    | What it is                                                |
| ---------------- | -------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
| `run` (+ `args`) | `<script>` or `<script type="module">` | A self-contained function, called with `args`.            |
| `json`           | `type="application/json"`              | Data for code on the page (`JSON.parse(el.textContent)`). |
| `ld`             | `type="application/ld+json"`           | Structured data for search engines.                       |
| `importmap`      | `type="importmap"`                     | Bare specifiers for the module scripts of the page.       |
| `speculation`    | `type="speculationrules"`              | The pages the browser may prefetch or prerender.          |

Attributes sit in the same object. `id`, `nonce` and any `data-*` work on every kind. `true` writes a bare attribute. `false`, `null` and `undefined` leave it out. Values are escaped. The object spreads, so a preset is a plain object: `script({ ...base, run })`.

A `run` script is CLASSIC by default. It runs where it stands, before the parser goes on. Thus in `<head>` it runs before the first paint. `type: 'module'` gives it its own scope and `import()`, and it runs after the document is parsed. A module also takes `async: true` (run as soon as it is ready), and `imports`: modules it needs, by name and URL (`imports: { carousel: url }`). They are written as static `import * as` lines before the call, and `run` gets them as ONE object first, then its `args`: `run: ({ carousel }, n) => …`. Declare a module there, and never `import()` it inside `run`: a bundler rewrites code it compiles (Vite's SSR transform turns `import()` into a helper the browser does not have), but it cannot touch a string. `script()` throws when it sees such a rewrite. The first paint does not wait for a module. Thus code that must apply before paint stays a classic script. `async` and `defer` do nothing on an inline classic script, so TypeScript refuses them there. (`blocking="render"` does not hold the paint for an inline module either, so it is not offered.) TypeScript also refuses `args` that do not match the parameters of `run`, an arg that JSON would change (a `Date`, a `Map`, a function), and a data script with code or with its own `type`.

The function is inlined through `Function.prototype.toString`. Thus it must be **self-contained**: only browser globals, no imports, and no closed-over variables (they do not exist at runtime). An arrow, a `function`, or a method in the object (`{ run() { … } }`) all work. For values that you would otherwise close over, pass them in `args`. They are JSON-serialized, and given to the function as parameters. Each `<` in an arg or in a data payload is escaped. In the code, each `</script` and each `<!--` is escaped (a `<!--` followed by `<script` would make the page swallow everything after the tag). Thus nothing can break out of the tag.

## `ogygia/vite`

```ts
import { ogygia } from 'ogygia/vite';
```

`ogygia(options)` is the Vite plugin. It runs before `sveltekit()`. Each option is optional. The root of the options is the region system itself: its defaults (`visible`), its named presets (`presets`), and how long its holes' URLs live (`ttl`, `maxPageAge`). Beside them sits the client router.

```ts
ogygia({
  visible: { margin: '120px' },
  presets: {
    demo: { wake: 'visible', margin: '200px' },
    frozenLive: { render: 'live', wake: 'load' }
  }
})
```

Every feature that lives in its own package (content, flags, freeze, federation, the server router, devtools) is a Vite plugin of its own, listed beside `ogygia()` in the `plugins` of your Vite config: `plugins: [ogygia(), flags(), freeze({ ttl: 600 }), sveltekit()]`. `ogygia()` finds them there. Each plugin keeps its own settings, so a region preset can never hold content config, and the reverse. See [Plugins](/docs/reference/plugins).

The old `regions: { … }` wrapper is a build error that names the new spelling. Its keys moved to the root, unchanged.

### `visible`

Sets the default `rootMargin` for `visible` islands and server islands. Thus they warm slightly before they enter the viewport.

```ts
ogygia({ visible: { margin: '200px' } })
```

There is no preload setting. The page carries each island's chunk list as data, and the runtime preloads an island's whole graph when it wakes, after the page has painted ([how the bytes arrive](/docs/regions/timing-nesting)). The old `preload` key is a build error.

### `router`

The SPA router is on by default, app-wide (see [SPA router](/docs/app/spa-router)). This is the one place where router config lives. There is no `<Router/>` component.

```ts
ogygia()                                       // router on, view transitions on, form continuity on
ogygia({ router: { viewTransitions: false } }) // SPA nav, but no view transitions
ogygia({ router: { forms: false } })           // SPA nav, but form fields don't survive navigation
ogygia({ router: false })                      // opt out of the SPA router entirely
```

`router: false` tree-shakes the router out of the generated runtime. Thus a load-only app never ships it. It also takes [form continuity](/docs/data-state/state-context#continuity-surviving-navigation) with it (there is no SPA navigation to survive). A single page can opt out of view transitions, with `<meta name="ogygia-router" content="plain">` in its head. The tag of the page wins over the global default.

### `barrels`

Barrel imports become leaf imports at transform time. A barrel is a module that only re-exports other modules (`index.ts`, a package's `components.ts`, an `export * from` fan-out). Importing one name from it drags the whole graph in: in dev every module behind it is transformed and served, in build the chunk graph follows the import graph before tree-shaking, and a package that is not `sideEffects: false` keeps all of it. With `barrels` on, `import { Button } from '$lib/components'` is rewritten to `import Button from '…/components/Button.svelte'` before Svelte or ogygia see the file, so the graph holds only the leaves the file uses.

```ts
ogygia({ barrels: true })                                  // every pure barrel in the project
ogygia({ barrels: { packages: ['@acme/ui'] } })            // dependencies too, by name / glob / regex
ogygia({ barrels: { force: ['$lib/registry'] } })          // an impure barrel: leaves moved, `import 'barrel'` kept
ogygia({ barrels: { keep: [/stores\/index\.ts$/] } })      // never touch these
```

What it does and does not do:

- **Project modules need no listing.** Every file outside `node_modules` is a candidate; purity decides. A barrel is _pure_ when every top-level statement is an import, a re-export, a type, or `export {}`. A module with its own code next to the re-exports is left alone unless listed in `force` (then its re-exported names move and a bare `import 'barrel'` stays, so its own code still runs).
- **Dependencies are opt-in** through `packages`, because Vite pre-bundles bare imports in dev: a leaf import that skips the optimizer while another importer gets the pre-bundled copy is two instances of one module. List a package when you know it is safe (an ESM component library, excluded from `optimizeDeps`).
- **Nested barrels, `export *` fan-outs, rename chains and `import … ; export { … }` bindings** are followed to the real leaf. A name two stars disagree on, a name the barrel does not export, and a name behind something unparsable (JSON, CommonJS, a `?raw` import) stay on the barrel import.
- **Import attributes and `type` modifiers ride along** (`with { type: 'json' }`, `import { type T }`). Sourcemaps are emitted.
- **Islands are untouched.** An import carrying region marks (`with { wake }`, `render`, `preset`, `region`, or the app's `importKeys`) is never rewritten, so a marked import means exactly what it meant before. A barrel binding is marked through `asRegion` or a mark in the barrel, as before, and an import whose binding feeds `import.meta.og.asRegion(X)` is left alone too: the island is keyed on that import.
- `import * as ns from 'barrel'`, `import 'barrel'` and dynamic imports are left as written.

Options: `packages`, `force`, `keep` (matchers: a package name or specifier prefix, a `*` glob, a regex, or a function of the resolved id), `importers: { include, exclude }` (which files are rewritten; default project files), `followPackages` (walk `export *` into dependencies while mapping a barrel; default `true`), `report` (the end-of-build report: `true` = the eight biggest barrels and a count of the rest, `'all'` = every barrel, `false` = silent), `debug` (log every rewrite).

The same pass is exported as a standalone Vite plugin for any app, ogygia or not:

```ts
import { debarrel } from 'ogygia/vite';
export default defineConfig({ plugins: [debarrel({ packages: ['@acme/ui'] }), svelte()] });
```

### `presets`

Named bundles of strategy + options, referenced from an import with `preset: 'name'`. A preset uses the same grammar as an inline import: `render` (the mode), `wake` (an island's schedule) or `fetch` (a hole's schedule), plus the tuning options that are not permitted inline (`margin`, `maxAge`, …):

```ts
ogygia({
  presets: {
    chart: { render: 'static', wake: 'visible', margin: '200px' },
    pricing: { render: 'deferred', fetch: 'load', maxAge: '1h' },
    ticker: { render: 'live', wake: 'idle', maxAge: '10m' }
  }
})
```

```svelte
<script>
  import Chart from './Chart.svelte' with { preset: 'chart' };
</script>
```

Fields: `render` (`'static'` | `'deferred'` | `'live'`), `wake` (an island's schedule), `fetch` (a hole's schedule), `margin`, `maxAge`, `onExpire`, `revalidate`, `keep`, `prefetch`.

#### `prefetch`: warm a `deferred` hole before it swaps

A hole's `fetch` is one moment: when its HTML is fetched **and** swapped in. `prefetch` splits them. The HTML is warmed into the frame store on the `prefetch` schedule (`'load'` | `'idle'` | `'visible'` | a media query); the swap still waits for `fetch`. An on-demand hole (`fetch: 'interaction'`) thus has its bytes before the first hover, and the gesture joins the warm frame instead of paying the origin round trip:

```ts
ogygia({ presets: { menu: { render: 'deferred', fetch: 'interaction', prefetch: 'idle', maxAge: '1h' } } })
```

```svelte
<script>
  import CountryPanel from './CountryPanel.svelte' with { render: 'deferred', fetch: 'interaction', prefetch: 'idle' };
</script>
```

`prefetch` rides inline or in a preset. It is `deferred`-only (an island's JS is preloaded by the runtime when it wakes, not by this). Never `'interaction'`: that is the swap, `fetch`'s job. Never the hole's own `fetch` schedule: it would add nothing. Without `prefetch`, an `interaction` hole keeps its default warm: the first hover fetches and swaps in one go.

#### `maxAge`: caching a `deferred` hole

A `render: 'deferred'` hole is **dynamic by default**: its endpoint is served `Cache-Control: no-store`. Thus it renders again on each request (a per-request clock, a personalized figure). `maxAge` opts a hole into a browser cache in its place, as seconds (a number), or as a duration string (`'30s'` | `'5m'` | `'1h'`):

```ts
ogygia({ presets: { pricing: { render: 'deferred', fetch: 'load', maxAge: '1h' } } })
```

The endpoint of that hole is served `Cache-Control: private, max-age=3600`. The `maxAge` is **signed into the URL of the hole**. Thus a harvested capability cannot be pointed again at a longer cache. `maxAge: 0` (or an omission) keeps the hole dynamic. With `render: 'live'`, `maxAge` is the client revalidate staleness in its place.

::: warning A bare number changes units by context
On a **deferred hole**, `maxAge: 60` means 60 **seconds**: it becomes an HTTP `max-age`, which is in seconds. On a **live / remount lake**, `maxAge: 60` means 60 **milliseconds**: it is client-side staleness, in ms, like each JS timer. The same key, two clocks. Duration strings (`'30s'`, `'5m'`, `'1h'`) mean the same thing in all locations. Thus prefer them, and the trap disappears.
:::

### `content()`

Turns on the markdown pipeline (mdsvex + Shiki + heading collection) for `.svx` / `.md` content collections. It is the plugin of the `@ogygia/content` package (`pnpm add @ogygia/content`), from `@ogygia/content/vite`. See [Content collections](/docs/content/collections).

```ts
import { content } from '@ogygia/content/vite';

plugins: [ogygia(), content({ markdown: {} }), sveltekit()]
```

**`presets`** are named markdown variants, with the same grammar as the presets of each subsystem. A [loader macro](/docs/macros/loaders) opts a full collection in with `{ preset: 'name' }`. The files of that collection compile through the config of the preset, merged over `markdown` (per setting key). Under the hood, each opted-in file is its own module _variant_. Thus the same file used by another collection (under another preset, or none) renders independently. There is nothing to conflict.

```ts
content({
  markdown: { overrides: true },                      // the defaults
  presets: { plain: { markdown: { overrides: false } } }  // a named variant
})
```

```ts
// blog.server.ts — the blog corpus compiles through the `plain` preset
loader: import.meta.og.loader.folder('../content/blog', { preset: 'plain' })
```

`presets` requires `markdown` to be set (the base that each preset merges over). The name must be a literal string. An unknown name is a build error that lists the configured names.

### `importKeys`

Extra import-attribute keys to treat as ogygia directives, for interop with other tooling that reads import attributes.

### `rateLimit`

Guard the signed island endpoint against abuse: requests per window per client.

### `sessionCookie`

Name / options for the session cookie that ogygia uses to scope signed capabilities.

### `ttl`

How long a signed region capability URL stays valid, in seconds (default `3600`, clamped to 60–86400). Keep it short: a harvested URL ages out.

Holes outlive it where they have to. Prerendered and frozen pages sign their holes for the life of the deploy. On a page whose load marks it shared-cacheable, for example `setHeaders({ 'cache-control': 'public, s-maxage=604800' })`, an anonymous hole is signed for the page's cache life. A hole on an older cached copy renews itself when it wakes: the endpoint re-signs a URL it minted, anonymous holes only, up to [`maxPageAge`](#ogygiavite-maxpageage) after it expired. A `cache-control` header a handle sets after the render is invisible to the mint, so set it from a load.

### `maxPageAge`

The oldest a cached copy of a page can be and still get its holes, in seconds (default 8 days). It sets how long after a hole's URL expired the hole can still renew itself. Past it, the hole keeps its fallback, and its request answers `403` with `x-ogygia-deny: renew-window`. `0` turns renewal off.

Raise it when your CDN keeps pages longer than 8 days and the page does not say so in its `cache-control` (a TTL set in the CDN's own configuration):

```ts
ogygia({ maxPageAge: 30 * 24 * 3600 });
```

It is not a longer `ttl`. A renewed URL still lives only `ttl`, and a hole bound to a session never renews. A renewable URL replays the props signed into it, so keep a hole's props to routing keys (a locale, a page) and read anything sensitive at render time.

### `OGYGIA_SECRET`

The HMAC secret that signs `render: 'deferred'` capability URLs. Set it in the environment, for stable signatures across a deployment. Otherwise, a per-build key is generated. Every deployment that serves your pages or your islands endpoint needs the same value at runtime: a hole signed by one key and checked by another is refused (`x-ogygia-deny: foreign-key`).

A hole's URL signs the component it renders, so a page cached across a deploy keeps its holes as long as their components still exist: a changed preset, `wake` or build-time `OGYGIA_SECRET` does not matter. A hole whose component was moved, renamed or removed is refused (`x-ogygia-deny: unknown-id`) and keeps its fallback.

Server islands and `render: 'live'` need the [server handle](/docs/start/install) installed, to serve the endpoint.

## `ogygia/server`

```ts
import * as ogygia from 'ogygia/server';
export const handle = ogygia.handle();
```

`handle(options)` is the SvelteKit server handle. It serves the signed region endpoint (per-hole loads, and the single-flight navigation batch), and injects client seeds.

| Option                                  | What it does                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |
| --------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `endpoint`                              | The region endpoint's path. Default `/__ogygia__`.                                                                                                                                                                                     |
| `transform(html, { kind, csr, event })` | Post-processes every document (`kind: 'document'`) and every region answer (`kind: 'region'`), and may reshape Svelte-owned markup: ogygia restores it before anything hydrates. See [Server transforms](/docs/app/server-transforms). |

## `ogygia/markup`

```ts
import { scanRegions, liftRegions, restoreRegions, transformMarkup, restore } from 'ogygia/markup';
```

Kit-free and import-free: it runs in the non-SvelteKit half of a monorepo, or in a test.

| Export                                                 | What it is                                                                                                               |
| ------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `scanRegions(html)`                                    | Yields every region in a document (island, lake or hole), with its span and attributes.                                  |
| `liftRegions(html)` · `restoreRegions(shell, regions)` | Lift the regions out around a foreign SSR pass, and splice them back with the marks the pass stamped.                    |
| `transformMarkup(html, transform, { kind, csr, dev })` | The server round trip of `handle({ transform })`: mark, your transform, settle.                                          |
| `checkScript(check)`                                   | The dev check's payload (`settled.check` with `dev: true`), to put in front of the markup so `restore` checks each host. |
| `localizeMarks(block)`                                 | Renumbers a block's marks from 0 for a render cache key; `.back(rendered)` puts the document's ids back.                 |
| `restore(root)`                                        | The browser half, on a parsed document or fragment (jsdom included).                                                     |

## `ogygia/app`

Island-safe replacements for `$app/navigation` (a csr=false page has no Kit client):

`goto` · `invalidate` · `invalidateAll` · `preloadData` · `preloadCode` · `beforeNavigate` · `afterNavigate` · `disableScrollHandling`

Prefer these over `$app/navigation` inside islands. See [SPA router](/docs/app/spa-router).

## `@ogygia/content`

```ts
import { content, markdown, json, blocks, glob, mapRaw } from '@ogygia/content';
```

| Export                                   | What it is                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |
| ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `content({ loader, schema, relations })` | Defines a collection. `Data` is inferred from `schema`, `Meta` from the loader, with no annotations. `refs` / `get` / `groups`; wire remotes (`list`, `live`) through `withRemotes()`. See [Content collections](/docs/content/collections). |
| `markdown` · `json` · `blocks`           | Source builders: `content({ loader: markdown(import.meta.glob(…)) })`. `markdown` provides `meta.headings`.                                                                                                                                  |
| `blocks.resolve(tree, registry)`         | Server-side helper: turns a block tree that you hold into region nodes, for a no-collection recomposer recipe. See [Blocks](/docs/content/blocks).                                                                                           |
| `glob` · `mapRaw` · `defineSource`       | Source primitives to build custom loaders (a CMS, an API). See [Content collections](/docs/content/collections).                                                                                                                             |
| `parseSchema`                            | Lower-level helper. `parseFrontmatter` lives on `@ogygia/content/markdown`.                                                                                                                                                                  |

An entry is `{ id, data, meta, body, rel, backlinks }`: `data` (schema output), `meta` (source-derived, for example `{ headings }`), `body` (a held region). Types: `Entry`, `ContentEntry`, `ContentHandle`, `Source`, `Heading`, `SchemaLike`.

## `ogygia/events` · `ogygia/insights`

`ogygia/events` is the event layer: the typed stream of what the framework did (island wakes, server renders, navigations), plus the bus and sink helpers (`add_sink`, `snapshot`, `clear`). It is compiled in only when a plugin asks for it (the devtools plugin does). `ogygia/insights` is the analysis engine the dock, the profiler and the test helpers share.

## Plugin packages

Each feature below is its own package. Install it, and list its plugin once beside `ogygia()` in your Vite config. See [Plugins](/docs/reference/plugins).

| Package              | Plugin                           | Imports in app code                                                                            |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `@ogygia/content`    | `content({ markdown, presets })` | `@ogygia/content`, `/remote`, `/markdown`, `/docs-shell`, `/types`                             |
| `@ogygia/flags`      | `flags()`                        | `flag`, `decide` from `@ogygia/flags`; `openfeature`, `ofrep` from `@ogygia/flags/openfeature` |
| `@ogygia/freeze`     | `freeze({ ttl, default })`       | `freeze` and the store and CDN adapters from `@ogygia/freeze`                                  |
| `@ogygia/federation` | `federation()`                   | `federate`, `mount` from `@ogygia/federation`                                                  |
| `@ogygia/router`     | `router()`                       | `routes`, `page`, … from `@ogygia/router`; `api` from `@ogygia/router/api`                     |
| `@ogygia/devtools`   | `devtools({ dock, profiler })`   | `@ogygia/devtools/profiler`, `/profiler/client`, `/profiler/storage/*`                         |
| `@ogygia/testing`    | none (a test helper)             | `@ogygia/testing`                                                                              |
| `@ogygia/cli`        | none (the command)               | `npx ogygia …`                                                                                 |
